# The pinned serving config for google/gemma-4-12B-it — the SINGLE source for the warm
# run, the baked image ENV/CMD, and the verify run. Every value here is part of the cubin
# cache key (it changes which programs exist / their shapes), so the warm and the released
# image must read the same file. That file is read BOTH ways — `include`d by the Makefile and
# `source`d by warm.sh/verify.sh — so the syntax is the intersection: `VAR=value`, one per
# line, and any value containing spaces MUST be double-quoted (bash would otherwise run its
# second word as a command). Make keeps those quotes in the value, so the Makefile strips
# them once into SERVE_GPU_NAME; nothing else here needs quoting.
#
# The filename IS the model slug (model_slug.sh) — `make serve-image MODEL=<id>` resolves
# models/<slug>.env, so onboarding a model is a new file here and nothing else.
#
# Pinned to the blog-article benchmark protocol (re-measured 2026-07-26, RTX 5090), with
# one deliberate change: --max-model-len 16384 rather than the article's 8448.
#
# VERIFIED AT 16384 (2026-07-31, RTX 5090, emmy c92d5956): the headroom boot at this exact
# config (bucket 32, mnbt 4128, util 0.97) reached health, completed the bench, and logged
# no engine fatal. The PUBLISHED image is still the 8448 warm — re-warm before releasing
# from this file. The ceiling is KV, not the compiler: the measured pool is 25,131 tokens
# at util 0.97, so 16384 leaves room for ~1.5 full-length sequences, and a max-model-len at
# or above ~25k fails vLLM's KV min-fit check at boot. Gemma-4-12B's full native context
# does not fit a 32 GB card at FP16 — it needs roughly 5x this pool.
#
# The rest below is the article protocol: the instruction-tuned checkpoint, decode bucket 32 (the tuned default of the
# decode-goldens sessions; the 64-stream point's EMMY_GEN_DECODE_BUCKET=64 knob is a
# run-time override that falls back to the full cubin-cached compile), and
# --max-num-batched-tokens 4128 = the dynamic-dim cap + the decode bucket — the
# rider headroom the chunk+decode twin row split covers (emmy serve's default at
# this rev; headroom-verified on-card: KV 25,131 tokens, bench 256/256, no engine
# fatal, at the 8448 shape). Util 0.97: the plugin's
# device residents (embed table + norm) are claimed BEFORE vLLM profiling, so they
# must sit inside the utilization budget — at 0.90 the KV cache no longer fits.
SERVE_MODEL=google/gemma-4-12B-it
SERVE_MAX_MODEL_LEN=16384
SERVE_MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=4128
SERVE_GPU_MEM_UTIL=0.97
# 32 = decode twin on at the tuned bucket; 0 disables.
SERVE_DECODE_BUCKET=32
# The card this config was headroom-swept and warmed on. Every cache-key input (the
# live-probed featurization, the golden picks, the memory headroom) is card-specific, so a
# warm on a different GPU bakes a cache the released image can never hit — warm.sh and
# verify.sh check the live card against this and refuse to run on a mismatch.
SERVE_GPU="NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090"
# Extra serving shapes to bake execution-plan packs for, beside the pinned one above:
# `<decode_bucket>:<prefill_bucket>:<max_num_batched_tokens>[:fm]`, empty field = pinned value.
# These are the per-workload knobs the article's recipes actually deploy, and without a pack
# each costs ~12 min of compiler frontend on EVERY boot of that lane (against ~2 min for a
# pack hit) — measured 2026-08-01, where 24 of 28 benchmark cells missed. The two `fm` rows
# exist because the precision gate is part of the pack key: a FAST_MATH boot can never hit a
# standard-lane pack. Cost is warm time only; packs are a few MB each.
#   8:2048:2056  — the 4K/4K c=4 and c=8 lanes (chunk quantum + rider headroom)
#   64::4096     — the 256/256 c=64 lane
#   192::4096    — the MTP c=64 depth-2 verify bucket
SERVE_WARM_SHAPES="8:2048:2056 64::4096 64:2048:2112 192::4096 :::fm 64::4096:fm 64:2048:2112:fm 8::4104 32:2048:2080"
